  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 314 PM CDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nThe first round of showers has moved into the area this \nafternoon but have remained mainly light to moderate as far as \nrainfall rates go (under 0.5\"/hr). The rain and widespread cloud\ncover have also helped cool temperatures back into the 70s.\n\nThis initial band of showers may decrease in coverage for a few\nhours here late this afternoon before coverage increases again \nheading into the evening as the center of the low gets closer \nto the area (currently centered over Iowa). Instability is slow \nto increase with this system which suggests that the lightning\ncoverage will remain low through this evening though a rogue \nlightning strike or two can't be ruled out prior to 9 PM. The \npotential for isolated embedded thunderstorms then increases \nfrom west to east through the late evening and continues \novernight (30-40% chance). Based on the favored track of the low\nand a collective assessment of the available model guidance, the \nthreat for localized flooding this evening through the night is \ngreatest north of I-80 and toward northwest Illinois and the \nWI/IL state line.\n\nThere may be a brief lull in coverage in the morning on \nSaturday (though that is not consistent among all the guidance),\npotentially mixed with reduced visibility in drizzle and/or \nfog. Confidence is higher that shower coverage increases \nthrough midday near and south of a Waukegan to Rochelle line,\nespecially across much of the Chicago metro area. This is the\nperiod we are most concerned about a renewed threat of lightning\nas well as heavy rainfall (rates in excess of 1-2\"/hr) which \nmay lead to localized flooding. Concern is greatest in urban\nareas and in any areas that experienced flooding from storms\nearlier in the week (if showers/storms happen to stall or\nredevelop over the same areas). We will also have to monitor \nfor potential funnel clouds south of the Illinois/Kankakee River\nValley where there is an area of higher low-level instability \ncloser to the center of the surface low. Heading into the \nevening hours on Saturday the northern deformation axis of the \nlow (and associated area of greater shower coverage and higher \nrainfall rates) will likely focus south and east of I-55 into \nnorthwest Indiana and east central Illinois.\n\nConfidence in if and where localized pockets or corridors of\nrainfall amounts in excess of 2-3 inches occurs in the local\narea remained too low to issue a Flood Watch with the afternoon\nforecast update. Have instead issued a Hydrologic Outlook (ESF)\nadd a bit more visibility to the flooding potential in addition\nto our graphical messaging. Will continue to monitor trends\nclosely in case a Flood Watch is needed with later updates.\n\nWhile some lingering showers can't be ruled out into portions \nof northwest Indiana into Sunday, cooler and dry conditions are\nthen favored during the first part of next week with gradually \nincreasing temperatures back into the 80s by TUE-WED. An upper \nlevel wave and attendant surface front may swing through the \narea toward midweek bringing renewed shower/storm chances though\nconfidence in the details remains low in that timeframe.\n\nPetr",
